---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyTimeConvert from '@/components/tools/datetime/CalcMoneyTimeConvert.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="military-time-converter">
  <CalcMoneyTimeConvert client:load variant="military" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The rule, in one sentence</h2>
    <p>
      From 1 PM on, <strong>add twelve</strong> to get military time. From 1300 on,
      <strong>subtract twelve</strong> to get PM time. Times before noon keep the same number with a
      leading zero. The only two special cases are midnight and noon.
    </p>

    <h2>The full clock</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Military</th>
            <th>12-hour</th>
            <th>Military</th>
            <th>12-hour</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>0000</td><td>12:00 AM (midnight)</td><td>1200</td><td>12:00 PM (noon)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0100</td><td>1:00 AM</td><td>1300</td><td>1:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0200</td><td>2:00 AM</td><td>1400</td><td>2:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0300</td><td>3:00 AM</td><td>1500</td><td>3:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0400</td><td>4:00 AM</td><td>1600</td><td>4:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0500</td><td>5:00 AM</td><td>1700</td><td>5:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0600</td><td>6:00 AM</td><td>1800</td><td>6:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0700</td><td>7:00 AM</td><td>1900</td><td>7:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0800</td><td>8:00 AM</td><td>2000</td><td>8:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0900</td><td>9:00 AM</td><td>2100</td><td>9:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1000</td><td>10:00 AM</td><td>2200</td><td>10:00 PM</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1100</td><td>11:00 AM</td><td>2300</td><td>11:00 PM</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Midnight and noon, the confusing part</h2>
    <p>
      AM means <em>ante meridiem</em>, before midday. PM means <em>post meridiem</em>, after midday.
      Midday is neither, and midnight is the boundary between two days. The convention that 12 PM is
      noon and 12 AM is midnight is arbitrary and often remembered wrong, so a hotel checkout "at
      12 AM" causes confusion.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 24-hour clock removes the ambiguity, so hospitals, airlines, emergency services, railways
      and the military use it. Midnight at the start of a day is 0000. Some organisations write 2400
      for midnight at the end of a day, and both mean the same instant. Written schedules usually use
      0000 for a start and 2359 for an end, so nothing is ambiguous.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is spoken</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>0600</code>: "zero six hundred" or "oh six hundred".</li>
      <li><code>1430</code>: "fourteen thirty".</li>
      <li><code>1000</code>: "ten hundred hours". There is no "o'clock" and no colon.</li>
      <li><code>0007</code>: "zero zero zero seven", seven minutes past midnight.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Strictly, "hundred hours" is for whole hours only. "Hours" after a time with minutes, such as
      "fourteen thirty hours", is common in films and rare in practice.
    </p>

    <h2>Time-zone letters</h2>
    <p>
      Military and NATO usage often adds one letter for the time zone, so a time is unambiguous
      anywhere on earth. Z ("Zulu") is UTC, the most common, in aviation, shipping and log files. J
      ("Juliet") means local time where the reader is. The letters A through M (except J) are east of
      Greenwich, and N through Y are west.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page converts notation, not offsets. <code>1430Z</code> and <code>1430</code> local are
      different instants, and to convert between them you need a time zone converter.
    </p>

    <h2>The 24-hour clock outside the military</h2>
    <p>
      Most of the world calls it "the clock". It is standard civilian notation across continental
      Europe, Latin America, and much of Asia and Africa, and it is the ISO 8601 standard for written
      time. The "military" label comes mostly from American English, because the US, UK, Canada,
      Australia and a few other English-speaking countries kept 12-hour notation in everyday use.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is one real difference: the civilian 24-hour clock uses a colon (<code>14:30</code>) and
      the military convention does not (<code>1430</code>). The input above accepts both, and the
      result shows both.
    </p>

    <h2>Converting a list</h2>
    <p>
      Paste a column of times, and each line converts to the other notation: 24-hour entries become
      AM/PM, and AM/PM entries become 24-hour. So a mixed roster or log becomes consistent. Minutes
      after midnight show next to each line, for sorting or for arithmetic in a spreadsheet.
    </p>

    <h2>No network, no upload</h2>
    <p>
      This military time converter is static HTML with a small JavaScript island and makes no external
      requests, so a pasted duty roster or shift log stays on your device. It works offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
